Implement user story AMSD-2041: [GO, UP, MX] Live Preview of Content in CMS

## Story Description
[GO, UP, MX] Live Preview of Content in CMS — Enable real-time content preview so that editors editing content in Contentstack CMS see their changes reflected immediately in the running application without a full page reload.

## Acceptance Criteria
- 


## Root Cause Analysis & Prescribed Fix (AUTHORITATIVE — start here, do not re-trace)
A code investigation already traced this bug to its cause and prescribed the minimal fix below. This is the plan of record. Apply it; do NOT re-read the whole codebase to re-derive it.

The Acceptance Criteria above describe the desired END BEHAVIOR to VERIFY — they are NOT an implementation blueprint. Do not re-architect, split values, or add new fields/abstractions to satisfy an AC literally when the prescribed minimal fix already makes that AC pass. Implement the fix below; the ACs are how you check you got it right.

HARD RULES:
- Make the SMALLEST change that fixes the root cause. Fewer lines of code is always better.
- REUSE existing functions. Before writing any new helper, search the repo for an existing util/parser/formatter that already does what you need (use the CodeGraph tool documented below) and call it. Writing novel code when a helper already exists is a defect to be rejected in review.
- **src/context/ContentstackContext.tsx** (`ContentstackProvider`): This is the single source that COMPUTES/PROVIDES all CMS content to the entire app (165+ renderers, 236+ useContent consumers). It currently freezes content into an immutable useMemo value, so no consumer can ever see updated content without a full page reload. Live Preview requires this provider to accept runtime content updates — it is the CAUSE (static content) not a SYMPTOM (any individual component not updating).
  - **Minimal fix:** Replace useMemo with useState so the content object can be updated at runtime. Change lines 56-61 from `const context = useMemo(() => ({ content: defaultContent }), [defaultContent])` to `const [content, setContent] = useState<IContentstackContent>(defaultContent)`. Then add a useEffect that subscribes to the Contentstack Live Preview SDK's `onContentUpdate` callback (from `@contentstack/live-preview-sdk`) and calls `setContent` with the updated payload. The Provider value becomes `{ content }` directly. This single change propagates live updates to all 236+ consumers without touching any component.


## Verification Criteria (what a tester will CONFIRM — your change must satisfy every one)
These are observable checks, derived from the acceptance criteria and description. They describe WHAT is observed, not how to build it. Make the minimal change that makes all of these true; your accompanying test should assert them:
- When an editor changes and saves/publishes content in Contentstack CMS, the running application displays the updated content on the currently open page without the tester reloading the browser tab.
- After an editor updates content in Contentstack CMS, the change is visible across all areas of the application that render that content, not just a single page or component.
- When an editor edits content in Contentstack CMS, the updated content appears in the running application while the page URL remains unchanged and the user's in-app state (e.g., scroll position, open dialogs, form inputs) is preserved.
- Before the editor makes any CMS change, the application continues to render the last-known content correctly, confirming that live preview does not break normal content display.
- When live preview is no longer receiving updates (e.g., the editor session ends), the tester observes that the page stops reflecting further CMS edits while the application continues to render the last-known content without a blank screen, error message, or crash.
- When the editor makes multiple successive content changes in Contentstack CMS, the tester observes each successive change reflected on the open page in sequence, without any reload between edits.
- An editor working in Contentstack CMS on a preview-enabled environment sees their changes reflected in the application, while a page not in a live-preview session continues to show the previously published content until reloaded.

